oaden"}, {"t": 3, "intent": "refute"}], "considered_alternatives": ["B1", "B3"]}}, "parent_branch": {"id": "B1", "label": "Gynecologic / Pelvic", "level": 1, "posterior": 0.4278169014084508, "danger": 0.95, "evidence_for": ["Hypovolemic shock and OCP use moderately support ectopic pregnancy as a cause of hemodynamic instability.", "Absence of Budd-Chiari syndrome's typical constitutional symptoms reduces its likelihood.", "High-risk factors (OCPs, prior urethritis) and hypotension strongly support ectopic pregnancy as a cause of shock, even without vaginal bleeding. Localized RUQ pain weakens this slightly but doesn't outweigh the risk profile.", "Coagulopathy risk factors (alcohol, weight loss) and RUQ pain provide moderate support for Budd-Chiari syndrome, though absence of jaundice/ascites reduces confidence. This creates plausible alternative explanation for shock."], "evidence_against": ["Combination of OCPs, alcohol, and rapid weight loss provides moderate support for Budd-Chiari syndrome.", "Weight loss and alcohol use provide minimal support for biliary obstruction, but lack of jaundice reduces its likelihood.", "Chronic alcohol use increases pancreatic concern, but absence of epigastric/back pain reduces its likelihood.", "Localized RUQ pain without vaginal bleeding weakens ectopic pregnancy consideration.", "Absence of gynecologic-specific signs (vaginal bleeding, shoulder pain) weakens ectopic pregnancy consideration, while RUQ focus slightly supports Budd-Chiari. Both branches remain in play.", "Missing Budd-Chiari's hallmark features (jaundice/ascites) weakens its probability, suggesting other causes of shock and RUQ pain might be more likely despite other risk factors.", "Atypical RUQ localization and absence of vaginal/shoulder signs weakens gynecologic source of shock, but ectopic implantation remains possible due to high-risk sexual activity."], "unresolved_questions": [], "askable_discriminators": ["History of amenorrhea or vaginal bleeding", "Posi